  "title": "Chapter 48: Budget and Taxation in India",
  "order": 48,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "j1z4b9e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Budget"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Under Article 112 of the Indian Constitution, the Union Budget is officially termed the Annual Financial Statement.",
        "The Financial Year in India begins on April 1 and ends on March 31 of the following year.",
        "Budgetary receipts are categorized into Revenue Receipts (non-liability creating) and Capital Receipts (liability creating or asset reducing).",
        "Goods and Services Tax (GST) represents a major indirect tax reform implemented across India on July 1, 2017.",
        "A Budget Deficit occurs when government total expenditure exceeds total receipts in a financial year."
      ],
      "id": "xs2e5rv"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Financial Year",
          "value": "April 1 - March 31",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "Constitutional Provision",
          "value": "Article 112",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "GST Rollout Date",
          "value": "July 1, 2017",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "Tax Categories",
          "value": "Direct & Indirect",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "ov6nlb7"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h5yx5vm",
      "content": "The budget is the annual financial statement of the government showing estimated revenue and expenditure for a financial year. In India, the Union Budget is presented by the Finance Minister in Parliament. According to Article 112 of the Constitution, the budget is known as the Annual Financial Statement."
    },
    {
      "id": "jcx8lpz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Financial Year in India"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kcc9re1",
      "content": "The financial year in India runs from 1 April to 31 March of the next year."
    },
    {
      "id": "zbwyz03",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Objectives of Government Budget"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "7t2fbdn",
      "content": "• Economic growth and development"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "k3nj1bn",
      "content": "• Reduction of poverty"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vqdei9m",
      "content": "• Employment generation"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "b2zdfht",
      "content": "• Price stability"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "7mfxohu",
      "content": "• Reduction of income inequality"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "syqh4y3",
      "content": "• Infrastructure development"
    },
    {
      "id": "icp926t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Components of Government Budget"
    },
    {
      "id": "wcwhawp",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Component</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Revenue Budget</p>",
            "<p>Includes revenue receipts and revenue expenditure</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Capital Budget</p>",
            "<p>Includes capital receipts and capital expenditure</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q2qqcyn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Revenue Receipts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0jjctx7",
      "content": "Revenue receipts are income received by the government without creating liabilities."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Chapter 48: Budget and Taxation in India",
      "id": "szoqwd8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z2hk9mh",
      "content": "Types:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nvsig42",
      "content": "• Tax Revenue – Income tax, GST, corporate tax"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sbwm58r",
      "content": "• Non-Tax Revenue – Fees, fines, dividends"
    },
    {
      "id": "2hy0kae",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Capital Receipts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j1fnleg",
      "content": "Capital receipts create liabilities or reduce government assets."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m7w91c9",
      "content": "Examples:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "k5pawbl",
      "content": "• Borrowings"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "u5imoqu",
      "content": "• Disinvestment"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "2apok85",
      "content": "• Recovery of loans"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Revenue Receipts",
        "Capital Receipts"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Liability Impact",
          "Neither create liability nor reduce assets",
          "Create liability or reduce assets"
        ],
        [
          "Nature",
          "Regular and recurring",
          "Irregular and non-recurring"
        ],
        [
          "Examples",
          "Tax revenues, interest receipts, dividends",
          "Market borrowings, recovery of loans, disinvestment"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "6jrp1kk"
    },
    {
      "id": "ds6o0l4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Government Expenditure"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9mjfsfb",
      "content": "Government expenditure is divided into revenue expenditure and capital expenditure."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o4zrqrj",
      "content": "Revenue Expenditure Examples: Salaries, subsidies, interest payments."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e7aco78",
      "content": "Capital Expenditure Examples: Infrastructure projects, roads, dams."
    },
    {
      "id": "m7vl2eu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Types of Taxes"
    },
    {
      "id": "q5p9rh8",
